        <body>
            <div xml:id="d207">

                <pb facs="0412" n="1066" sameAs="frus:frus1875v02/pg1066"/>

                <head>No. 506.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Schuyler to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">St.
                                Petersburg</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1875-05-18"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 18, 1875</date>.
                        (Received June 3.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 118.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have been informed that shortly before
                    the departure of the Emperor, the German Ambassador presented at the Russian
                    foreign office a proposition for the revision of the Russian laws with regard to
                    offenses against the peace and quiet of other states, the question started by
                    the exchange of notes with Belgium, referred to in my dispatches Nos. 105 and
                    109.</p>
                <p>The Russian government did not, at the time, seem disposed to meet the wishes of
                    Germany in this matter, not expecting any practical advantage to result.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>EUGENE SCHUYLER.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
